WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.730
In November of eighteen twelve, more than a hundred thousand people stood waiting on the bank of a river.

2
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.900
Ahead of them ran a current carrying blocks of ice.

3
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:12.870
Behind them and on both flanks were three separate Russian armies.

4
00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:21.320
The fate of an empire now rested in the hands of a few hundred men who waded chest-deep into the freezing water, and most of those men would never be warm again.

5
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.650
In mid-September of eighteen twelve, Moscow was burning.

6
00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:42.420
The first smoke rose only a few hours after Napoleon's soldiers entered the city, and within a few days whole districts of wooden houses had been reduced to ash.

7
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:53.590
Who started the fire is still debated today, but most historians believe that the city's governor, Rostopchin, personally arranged for arsonists and for the removal of the fire-fighting equipment,

8
00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:57.240
so that everything left behind for the French would be destroyed.

9
00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:02.330
From the windows of the Kremlin, Napoleon watched the flames paint the sky red.

10
00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:06.780
He had captured the heart of Russia, but that heart was no longer beating.

11
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:13.790
The army that had crossed the Neman River at the start of that summer, in June, was one of the largest Europe had ever seen.

12
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:21.600
Counting its reserves, this host of nearly six hundred thousand was called the Grande Armée, but it was not made up of Frenchmen alone.

13
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.770
In its ranks marched Poles, Germans, Italians, Dutchmen, Swiss, Croats and Portuguese.

14
00:01:28.770 --> 00:01:33.700
All the peoples of an empire had marched east at the will of a single man.

15
00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:40.390
By the time they reached Moscow, their numbers had already melted away to a little over a hundred thousand.

16
00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:47.880
Disease, heat, hunger and the bloody pitched battle at Borodino had exacted a price for every kilometer of the road.

17
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:53.930
Believing that Tsar Alexander would sue for peace, Napoleon waited in Moscow for five weeks.

18
00:01:53.930 --> 00:01:57.100
He sent letters, and no answer came.

19
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:02.030
At last, on the nineteenth of October, the army marched out of the burned city.

20
00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:10.480
The wagons were laden with plunder: gold-embroidered fabrics, church treasures, furs, paintings, candlesticks.

21
00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:13.970
The soldiers' knapsacks held more silver than bread.

22
00:02:13.970 --> 00:02:19.700
Before long, these burdens would be abandoned one by one at the roadside, in the snow.

23
00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.310
Napoleon first tried to head south, into lands that had not been plundered.

24
00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:35.160
But after clashing with Russian forces at Maloyaroslavets on the twenty-fourth of October, he was forced to change course, and the army began to retreat along the very road it had come by in early summer:

25
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:40.410
the old Smolensk road, already stripped bare, the villages around it burned.

26
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.740
That decision sealed the fate of the retreat.

27
00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.270
There was no food along the way.

28
00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:55.200
The horses were the first to die; the soldiers butchered them and roasted the meat over their fires, sometimes fighting over an animal that had not yet gone cold.

29
00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:01.570
As they passed Borodino, they walked among thousands of dead who had been left unburied two months before.

30
00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:05.220
In the first days of November, the snow began.

31
00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:10.630
From then on, the cold joined forces with hunger and gnawed at the army from within.

32
00:03:10.630 --> 00:03:16.200
Each morning, around the campfires, men were found who had frozen to death in the night.

33
00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:18.730
Discipline collapsed.

34
00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:25.260
Every day, more soldiers threw away their weapons, abandoned their units and struck out on their own.

35
00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:30.990
This leaderless mass became a human flood flowing behind and alongside the regular troops.

36
00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:37.840
Among them were the wounded, the sick, and the merchants, cooks, women and children who followed the army.

37
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:44.210
The Cossacks constantly tore away at the edges of this flood, taking prisoner or killing whoever fell behind.

38
00:03:44.210 --> 00:03:52.340
When the army reached Smolensk, the great supply depots it had been counting on had already been emptied by the looting of those who arrived first.

39
00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:58.390
Then, around Krasnoi, in the middle of November, came days of fighting with Russian forces.

40
00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:08.280
Marshal Ney fell so far behind with his rearguard that for a time he was believed dead; in the end, with only a handful of men, he crossed the icy Dnieper by night and rejoined the army.

41
00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:14.810
The number of regular soldiers still bearing arms at Napoleon's side had now dwindled to a few tens of thousands.

42
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:22.460
But ahead of the army lay a river that sat on the map as a thin blue line, one that hardly anyone had given much thought to: the Berezina.

43
00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:27.870
The Berezina flows from north to south through what is today Belarus, before joining the Dnieper.

44
00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:35.040
On its own, it is not considered a great river; at its crossing points it is only about one hundred meters wide.

45
00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.650
The real obstacle is the marshland that covers both its banks.

46
00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:47.300
Reaching the river, and getting away from the far bank, was possible only along long raised causeways built from tree trunks.

47
00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:55.430
The most sensible route for Napoleon's retreat westward, toward Vilnius, ran across the long wooden bridge at the town of Borisov.

48
00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:59.400
That bridge was the gateway to salvation for the entire army.

49
00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:07.530
What Napoleon did not know, or could not prevent even if he did, was that the Russians were closing in from three directions to shut that gateway.

50
00:05:07.530 --> 00:05:12.940
From the east, following on the army's heels, came Marshal Kutuzov's main army.

51
00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:20.750
The old commander preferred to let hunger and cold wear down his enemy rather than risk a great pitched battle, and he kept his distance as he pursued.

52
00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:25.360
From the north, General Wittgenstein's corps was marching down.

53
00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:33.330
And from the south, from a quarter no one had expected, came the Army of the Danube under Admiral Pavel Chichagov.

54
00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:36.340
There was a reason this army was here.

55
00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:41.110
Until the spring of that year, Russia had been at war with the Ottoman Empire.

56
00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:50.040
When the Treaty of Bucharest, signed in May of eighteen twelve, brought that war to an end, the Russian forces along the Danube were freed up and sent north.

57
00:05:50.040 --> 00:06:02.330
Chichagov, a naval man, was considered inexperienced in land campaigns, but the plan guiding him was clear: link up with Wittgenstein at the Berezina and trap Napoleon between the river and Kutuzov.

58
00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:06.780
On the sixteenth of November, Chichagov took Minsk.

59
00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:15.870
That alone was a heavy blow to Napoleon, because Minsk held vast supply depots stockpiled for the retreating army, and every one of them fell into Russian hands.

60
00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.880
The admiral then turned toward Borisov.

61
00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.130
The western end of the bridge was held by a Polish division under General Dąbrowski.

62
00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:28.100
On the twenty-first of November, there was a fierce clash.

63
00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:31.750
The Poles suffered heavy losses and fell back.

64
00:06:31.750 --> 00:06:35.720
The Russians took the bridgehead and the town.

65
00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:40.490
Napoleon learned the news on the road, as he marched with his army.

66
00:06:40.490 --> 00:06:44.460
He immediately sent Marshal Oudinot's corps to Borisov.

67
00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:52.430
On the twenty-third of November, Oudinot fell upon Chichagov's advance guard and drove them out of the town and across to the far bank of the river.

68
00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.720
But as the Russians withdrew, they set fire to the bridge behind them.

69
00:06:56.720 --> 00:07:02.130
The wooden bridge burned, along with the sections that stretched across the marshland.

70
00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:06.900
The town was back in French hands, but it no longer meant anything.

71
00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:11.350
On the opposite bank, Chichagov's army stood dug in and waiting.

72
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:15.160
Now picture Napoleon's situation on a map.

73
00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:19.290
Behind him, Kutuzov, a few days' march away.

74
00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:24.700
To the north, Wittgenstein, poised to fall on his right flank at any moment.

75
00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:30.270
Ahead of him, a river whose bridge had burned, and beyond it, a Russian army.

76
00:07:30.270 --> 00:07:39.360
With him, perhaps forty to fifty thousand men still able to carry arms, and trailing behind them perhaps as many again who were unarmed, starving, and sick.

77
00:07:39.360 --> 00:07:44.290
Military history offers few pictures of encirclement as stark as this one.

78
00:07:44.290 --> 00:07:50.180
At Russian headquarters, the capture of the French emperor was now being discussed as merely a matter of time.

79
00:07:50.180 --> 00:07:54.630
And nature, too, was playing an unexpected trick.

80
00:07:54.630 --> 00:08:01.000
In the last week of November, the weather briefly turned milder, and the bitter cold gave way to a thaw.

81
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:03.690
This kept the river from freezing.

82
00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:10.540
Had the Berezina frozen over in the hard frosts of a few weeks earlier, the army might simply have marched across it.

83
00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:15.310
But now the water was flowing, carrying great chunks of ice downstream.

84
00:08:15.310 --> 00:08:20.240
The ice was not solid enough to walk on, and the water was not warm enough to swim.

85
00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:22.610
The army needed a bridge.

86
00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:28.500
And only a few days earlier, they had burned the very materials to build one with their own hands.

87
00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.310
Let us go back a few days, to the town of Orsha.

88
00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:40.920
By the time Napoleon arrived there, around the twentieth of November, the heavy loads dragging along behind the army had become impossible to carry.

89
00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:44.890
Horses were dying, and cannon were being left by the roadside.

90
00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:55.420
The emperor made a decision: the bridging train, which carried the pontoons used for river crossings, would be burned, and the horses that pulled those wagons would be handed over to the artillery.

91
00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:57.950
The logic was understandable.

92
00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:03.840
In the dry days of summer, hauling heavy pontoons down the middle of the road had seemed like a luxury.

93
00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.770
The guns, on the other hand, were the army's last means of defense.

94
00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:12.900
In charge of the bridging train was General Jean-Baptiste Eblé.

95
00:09:12.900 --> 00:09:18.950
He was fifty-four years old, an artilleryman by training, a quiet and meticulous man.

96
00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:25.800
Under his command were the pontoniers, specialist soldiers trained to build pontoon and trestle bridges across rivers.

97
00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:29.930
They numbered around four hundred, and a large share of them were Dutch.

98
00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:36.620
These Dutch pontoniers had grown up in a country laced with rivers and canals, men well used to working with water.

99
00:09:36.620 --> 00:09:40.590
One of their companies was led by Captain George Diederich Benthien.

100
00:09:40.590 --> 00:09:43.120
Eblé obeyed the order.

101
00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.810
The pontoons were set on fire.

102
00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:50.260
But according to those same accounts, there was one thing he did not burn.

103
00:09:50.260 --> 00:10:00.790
On his own initiative, perhaps sensing that the rivers were not yet behind them, he held back two portable forges, two wagons of charcoal, and six wagons of tools.

104
00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:07.640
In those wagons were axes, adzes, saws, picks, nails, iron clamps, and ropes.

105
00:10:07.640 --> 00:10:13.530
He is also said to have ordered each of his men to carry a tool and a quantity of nails on his back.

106
00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:20.860
The pontoons were gone, but the hands that knew how to build a bridge, and the iron those hands would need, were still with the army.

107
00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:27.230
The whole story of the Berezina was built on this small act of disobedience, or perhaps of foresight.

108
00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:33.120
Once the Borisov bridge had burned, the question facing Napoleon was this: where to cross?

109
00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.370
The answer came from General Corbineau, one of Oudinot's advance cavalry commanders.

110
00:10:38.370 --> 00:10:47.300
Corbineau's brigade had earlier been operating in the north alongside another force, and had reached the river while searching for a way to rejoin the main army.

111
00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:53.990
A local peasant showed them a ford north of Borisov, near the village of Studianka, where horses could cross.

112
00:10:53.990 --> 00:10:59.720
Corbineau's cavalry rode across the river at that spot and sent word to Oudinot.

113
00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:06.250
At Studianka the river was narrow, and the far bank was somewhat higher and relatively firm.

114
00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:11.660
What is more, on the opposite side, a road ran west through the marshes toward Zembin.

115
00:11:11.660 --> 00:11:17.230
But there was a problem: Chichagov was also watching the points where the river could be crossed.

116
00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:24.240
If he realized the French had chosen Studianka, he could shift his army there and strangle the crossing before it had even begun.

117
00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:29.330
What Napoleon did at this moment is remembered as one of the cleverest deceptions of his career.

118
00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:34.420
He ordered Oudinot to make showy preparations south of Borisov, near Ukholodi.

119
00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:41.750
Soldiers were marched there, trees were cut down, and a great deal of noise was made, as if a bridge were being built.

120
00:11:41.750 --> 00:11:47.160
Among the local people, a rumor that the French would cross to the south was deliberately spread.

121
00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:55.930
According to some accounts, a few Jewish merchants were given false information about the crossing point, in the expectation that it would reach the Russian side.

122
00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:58.620
The deception worked.

123
00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:08.510
Chichagov was already inclined to believe that Napoleon would want to cross to the south, toward Minsk, and the warnings coming from Kutuzov pointed the same way.

124
00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:11.680
The admiral shifted his main force south.

125
00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:20.610
Opposite Studianka, around the village of Brili, he left only a small force under General Chaplitz, and most of that was later recalled as well.

126
00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:27.460
And so, across from the very point where Napoleon meant to cross, the far bank was left almost empty.

127
00:12:27.460 --> 00:12:32.710
On the evening of November twenty-fifth, Eblé and his men reached Studianka.

128
00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:36.360
They began tearing down the village's wooden houses.

129
00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:40.810
Beams, planks and posts were to be turned into bridge trestles.

130
00:12:40.810 --> 00:12:47.820
All through the night, in the darkness, they worked without lighting fires, so that the Cossacks on the far bank would not notice.

131
00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:51.630
Clamps and nails were hammered out at the blacksmiths' forges.

132
00:12:51.630 --> 00:13:02.000
The pontoneers lashed timbers together to build trestles: triangular or rectangular wooden frames that would stand upright in the water, with planks laid across their tops.

133
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:09.490
The material was wet, warped and uneven; logs pulled from village houses had never been cut for a bridge.

134
00:13:09.490 --> 00:13:11.700
But there was nothing else.

135
00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:15.830
The next morning, Napoleon came to Studianka himself.

136
00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:20.120
From a small rise on the riverbank, he watched the opposite shore.

137
00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:26.170
When he saw that the main weight of the Russian forces had shifted south, he ordered the crossing to begin.

138
00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:35.580
On the morning of November twenty-sixth, Corbineau's cavalry and a group of volunteers crossed to the far bank on rafts and on horseback and drove off the Cossacks there.

139
00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:39.390
Artillery batteries were set up on the shore.

140
00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:42.720
And the pontoneers went into the water.

141
00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:45.250
Try to picture what these men did.

142
00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:50.020
The water came up to their shoulders in some places, to their chests in others.

143
00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:55.110
The current carried chunks of ice that slammed into the trestles and into the men's bodies.

144
00:13:55.110 --> 00:14:02.120
The trestles had to be fixed to the riverbed, kept level, and then the beams had to be set on top and nailed down.

145
00:14:02.120 --> 00:14:05.770
The bottom was muddy, and the trestles kept sinking.

146
00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:12.620
The men would climb out of the water, shiver for a few minutes beside a fire on the bank, and then go back in.

147
00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:15.470
Some were swept away by the current.

148
00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:18.800
Some fainted from the cold and drifted off.

149
00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:21.650
But the work never stopped.

150
00:14:21.650 --> 00:14:27.380
According to witnesses, Eblé was on the bank among his men, encouraging them one by one.

151
00:14:27.380 --> 00:14:34.230
At around one o'clock, the first bridge, the northern one, built for infantry and cavalry, was finished.

152
00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:39.640
Oudinot's corps began crossing at once and established a bridgehead on the far bank.

153
00:14:39.640 --> 00:14:46.970
At around four in the afternoon, the second bridge, built more sturdily for guns and wagons, was also complete.

154
00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:50.460
The two bridges stood a few hundred meters apart.

155
00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:56.830
In the middle of an encirclement, in a single day, Napoleon's army had opened a road across the river.

156
00:14:56.830 --> 00:15:00.000
But the bridges were not permanent structures.

157
00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.890
The bridge carrying the heavy guns and wagons in particular was constantly being crushed under the load.

158
00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:10.820
That night, at around eight o'clock, some of the trestles collapsed.

159
00:15:10.820 --> 00:15:18.790
The pontoneers went back into the water, in the dark, in a current full of ice, and repaired them within a few hours.

160
00:15:18.790 --> 00:15:25.480
The following night, at two o'clock, the bridge broke again; toward morning it was repaired again.

161
00:15:25.480 --> 00:15:29.770
On the afternoon of November twenty-seventh, it collapsed once more.

162
00:15:29.770 --> 00:15:36.140
Eblé had divided his men into shifts; while one group worked, the other tried to recover by the fire.

163
00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:40.430
From every shift, there were men who never made it back for the next one.

164
00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:45.360
On November twenty-seventh, Napoleon crossed over with the Imperial Guard.

165
00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:51.730
In these first days the crossing was relatively orderly, because the bridges were reserved first for armed units.

166
00:15:51.730 --> 00:15:54.740
The real problem was the unarmed crowd.

167
00:15:54.740 --> 00:16:01.110
Thousands of people had gathered on the eastern bank, around Studianka, huddled around campfires.

168
00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:11.480
Many of them could have crossed at night, when the bridges stood empty, but they did not move, out of exhaustion, out of apathy, or out of fear that they would freeze if they left the fire.

169
00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:16.090
And when morning came, they all surged onto the bridges at once.

170
00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:19.580
Meanwhile, danger appeared from the north as well.

171
00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:26.430
Wittgenstein's troops had made contact with Marshal Victor's corps, which was serving as the rearguard on the eastern bank.

172
00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:32.800
On the night of November twenty-seventh, one of Victor's divisions, the division under General Partouneaux,

173
00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:39.650
lost its way in the dark while falling back from Borisov to Studianka and blundered into the middle of the Russian lines.

174
00:16:39.650 --> 00:16:43.620
Most of the division was surrounded and forced to surrender.

175
00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:47.430
November twenty-eighth was the bloodiest day of the Berezina.

176
00:16:47.430 --> 00:16:51.240
The battle raged on both banks of the river at once.

177
00:16:51.240 --> 00:17:01.770
On the western bank, Chichagov had realized he'd been deceived and had turned back from the south; in the forest between Brili and Stakhov, the troops of Oudinot and Ney tried to stop him.

178
00:17:01.770 --> 00:17:04.620
Oudinot was gravely wounded.

179
00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:15.630
In this fighting, the Swiss regiments of Oudinot's corps held a defense that lasted the entire day and turned again and again into bayonet charges; even when their ammunition ran out, they did not fall back.

180
00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:23.280
A charge by Polish troops and French cuirassiers along the edge of the snow-covered forest halted the Russian advance.

181
00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:33.170
On the eastern bank, meanwhile, Victor was trying to keep Wittgenstein away from the bridges with the German troops he had left, soldiers from Baden, Hesse and Berg.

182
00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:40.020
Just behind the ground where these men were fighting, a crowd tens of thousands strong was massed in front of the bridges.

183
00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:46.710
When the Russian guns came within range and opened fire on that crowd, the panic became a full catastrophe.

184
00:17:46.710 --> 00:17:50.360
People trampled one another trying to reach the bridges.

185
00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:57.210
Horses, wagons, the wounded, women and children were all forced toward the same narrow crossing.

186
00:17:57.210 --> 00:18:01.500
Those who fell from the edge of the bridge were swept away by the icy current.

187
00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:05.790
Some tried to swim across the river; very few made it.

188
00:18:05.790 --> 00:18:11.360
At the approaches to the bridges, heaps of dead people and animals made the crossing even harder.

189
00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:16.930
By evening, even Victor's troops had to clear a path through those heaps to reach the bridge.

190
00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:21.060
That night, what remained of Victor's corps crossed over.

191
00:18:21.060 --> 00:18:25.190
Still, thousands of people stayed on the eastern bank.

192
00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:30.440
Around the fires, beneath the wagons, they waited for morning in a numb stupor.

193
00:18:30.440 --> 00:18:34.730
Eblé had been ordered to set the bridges on fire early in the morning.

194
00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:41.580
If the Russians reached the bridges on the eastern bank, it would only be a matter of time before they were on Napoleon's heels.

195
00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.750
But the general put off carrying out that order.

196
00:18:44.750 --> 00:18:51.440
He sent his men into the crowd on the eastern bank, trying to wake people and persuade them to walk toward the bridges.

197
00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.490
Many did not get up.

198
00:18:53.490 --> 00:18:56.500
Some did, but too late.

199
00:18:56.500 --> 00:19:05.910
On the morning of November twenty-ninth, between eight and nine o'clock, Russian advance guards appeared on the heights of Studianka, and Eblé could wait no longer.

200
00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:08.760
The bridges were set alight.

201
00:19:08.760 --> 00:19:15.290
As the flames began to wrap around the planks, the people on the eastern bank began running toward the bridges.

202
00:19:15.290 --> 00:19:18.460
Some tried to cross over the burning bridges.

203
00:19:18.460 --> 00:19:21.630
Some threw themselves into the water.

204
00:19:21.630 --> 00:19:25.440
The thousands left behind fell into the hands of the Cossacks.

205
00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:33.090
The pontoneers had built those bridges with their own hands and kept them standing for three days from inside the water, at the cost of their lives.

206
00:19:33.090 --> 00:19:35.940
Now the same hands destroyed them.

207
00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:39.270
The losses at the Berezina are not known for certain.

208
00:19:39.270 --> 00:19:46.760
Historians' estimates, counting armed soldiers and the unarmed crowd together, range from twenty-five thousand to forty thousand.

209
00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:50.410
Russian losses are estimated at around ten thousand.

210
00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:59.340
It is said that when spring came and the ice on the river melted, hundreds of bodies, broken wagons and chests full of plunder emerged all along the banks.

211
00:19:59.340 --> 00:20:03.630
So was the Berezina a defeat, or a victory?

212
00:20:03.630 --> 00:20:07.280
The question has been argued for two hundred years.

213
00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:12.050
The Russian plan had been to capture Napoleon, and that never happened.

214
00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:18.580
The Emperor, his Guard and the armed core of his army slipped out from between three Russian armies and made it west.

215
00:20:18.580 --> 00:20:29.910
In Russian public opinion, the blame for this escape fell largely on Chichagov; the admiral spent the rest of his career in the shadow of that accusation, and in the end chose to leave Russia and live in Europe.

216
00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:35.960
The Grande Armée, on the other hand, largely ceased to exist as an army after the Berezina.

217
00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:39.610
Right after the crossing, the real winter set in.

218
00:20:39.610 --> 00:20:46.460
In the first days of December the temperature plunged to brutal lows, and whatever order was left on the road fell apart.

219
00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:52.830
On December third, on the road at Molodechno, Napoleon dictated his famous Twenty-Ninth Bulletin.

220
00:20:52.830 --> 00:20:59.200
It was the first official text to openly admit to the French people that the campaign had ended in disaster.

221
00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:05.890
When it was published in Paris, families across the country began to wonder what had become of their sons and husbands.

222
00:21:05.890 --> 00:21:13.380
The bulletin's final sentence would be remembered for years with bitter irony: it announced that the Emperor's health had never been better.

223
00:21:13.380 --> 00:21:20.070
On December fifth, at Smorgon, Napoleon left his army and set out secretly for Paris.

224
00:21:20.070 --> 00:21:28.200
When those left behind crossed back over the Neman in mid-December, they were a small remnant of the multitude that had crossed the same river in June.

225
00:21:28.200 --> 00:21:32.010
General Eblé did not live even a month after the Berezina.

226
00:21:32.010 --> 00:21:38.700
The days spent in the water, then the cold and the exhaustion that followed, took him just as they had taken his men.

227
00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:44.430
He died on December thirty-first, eighteen twelve, in Königsberg, today's Kaliningrad.

228
00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:52.240
Most of the roughly four hundred pontoneers under his command also lost their lives, either at the Berezina or in the weeks that followed.

229
00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:58.770
Many of them paid for the hours they had spent in the water by freezing to death soon after they climbed out onto the bank.

230
00:21:58.770 --> 00:22:03.860
Today Eblé's name is inscribed on the Arc de Triomphe in Paris.

231
00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:12.310
And in the Netherlands, the pontoneers of the Berezina are remembered as part of the nation's military memory, with commemorations held every year.

232
00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:18.360
Berezina's memory found a home not only in the countries of those who died there, but in other places too.

233
00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:25.690
In Switzerland, the memory of the regiments that fought in the Brili forest on November twenty-eighth lives on in a song.

234
00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:34.940
According to one account, that morning a Swiss lieutenant had the men around him sing an old German hymn before the battle, one that likens life to a journey made by night.

235
00:22:34.940 --> 00:22:42.110
In Switzerland the song is known as the Berezina Song, and it is still sung today at military ceremonies and funerals.

236
00:22:42.110 --> 00:22:45.920
But Berezina's most lasting legacy is a single word.

237
00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:59.010
In French, even today, to say that something has collapsed completely and that no hope remains, people say "c'est la Bérézina," meaning "this is a total Berezina." A crushing defeat in a football match,

238
00:22:59.010 --> 00:23:06.340
a company going bankrupt, an election campaign gone badly wrong: any of these can be a Berezina.

239
00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:15.590
Most of the French people who use the expression may not know that they are speaking the name of a distant Eastern European river, or what happened on its banks two hundred years ago.

240
00:23:15.590 --> 00:23:20.200
What is striking is that the expression carries only one side of the event.

241
00:23:20.200 --> 00:23:30.570
Napoleon himself, and some historians on his side, saw Berezina as a military success, because the encirclement had been broken and the army had escaped annihilation.

242
00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:33.420
Language refused to accept that.

243
00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:37.070
The people's memory did not hold on to those who escaped.

244
00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:43.280
It held on to the crowds before the bridges, the people running across burning planks, and the icy water.

245
00:23:43.280 --> 00:23:50.770
Today in Belarus, between Studyanka and Brili, near the place where the crossing was made, there are monuments.

246
00:23:50.770 --> 00:23:54.740
The river flows calmly, and the banks are still marshland.

247
00:23:54.740 --> 00:24:04.630
In twenty twelve, on the two hundredth anniversary of the event, people from France, Russia, Switzerland, the Netherlands and Poland held commemorations here together.

248
00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:10.360
Peoples who were once enemies bowed their heads on the banks of the same river, for the same dead.

249
00:24:10.360 --> 00:24:15.610
The story of Berezina has emperors, marshals and grand strategic moves.

250
00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:18.620
But none of these made the crossing possible.

251
00:24:18.620 --> 00:24:30.270
What made it possible were logs pulled from dismantled village houses, six wagons of tools that one commander refused to burn, and a few hundred men standing in a river full of ice, submerged up to their shoulders,

252
00:24:30.270 --> 00:24:34.080
clinging to one another's bodies to hold the trestles upright.

253
00:24:34.080 --> 00:24:37.410
Most of those men's names appear in no record.

254
00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:41.540
The bridges they built held for three days, and then they burned.

255
00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:47.910
But those three days were enough to change the fate of an army, an emperor, and tens of thousands of people.

256
00:24:47.910 --> 00:24:51.720
We have come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Tarih.

257
00:24:51.720 --> 00:24:54.250
Until we meet again in a new story.
